-+# 05 — Defense System (Towers)
-+
-+*Status: skeleton. Blocking: D-3 (enemy identity) before content; M4 planning.*
-+
-+## Thesis: gradient engineering (P-10)
-+Waves are emergent trail-followers, so towers act on the *field*, not only on targets:
-+repellent towers write negative scent, lures write attractants, and the strategic game is
-+sculpting the landscape pests navigate. Damage towers exist, but the maze is chemical.
-+Continuous with phase-2 counterplay (wash/stomp/dig — the player has been doing gradient
-+engineering by hand since phase 2).
-+
-+## Sections to fill
-+1. **Tower roster** — axes: field-writers (repellent, lure) vs. actors (projectile,
-+   trap) vs. sensors (watchtower extends player/bot awareness). Each tower's verb set is
-+   a player-verb subset where possible (P-9 spirit).
-+2. **Manual→autonomous progression** — the phase's own delegation arc `[Joint]`: e.g.
-+   towers start needing manual triggering → gain auto-fire → gain target priorities.
-+   What exactly the player stops doing, step by step (phase-3 stub question).
-+3. **Wave design pipeline** — waves scale with farm yield (the farm's success causes the
-+   siege). Authorability check `[Playtest]` (P-10 named risk): can emergent
-+   trail-following produce legible, fair waves, or do we need authored spine paths
-+   modulated by scent?
-+4. **Raid scheduling** — real-time interruption vs. scheduled (dawn/dusk) `[Joint]`
-+   (01-game-loops).
-+5. **Failure stakes** — `[Designer]` Q-22: what a lost wave costs — crops, towers;
-+   never progress-destroying (S-7's spirit carried forward).
-+6. **Space economics** — towers occupy farmable tiles; the defense-vs-yield tension is
-+   the phase's economy and the setup for D-7 (bots retiring towers — now part of the
-+   4→5 gate substance per P-4's ruling).
-+
-+## Constraints from decisions
-+Wave previews are fast-forward sims (S-5) — cheap to expose if design wants them.
-+Tower placement is tap-command (P-1). Enemy variety = scent-sensitivity axes (04).
